## Other

- [x] Add open recent files - the start page's list and RECENT in the EVA menu (2026-10-17)
- [ ] **Log file for end users** — the log goes to stderr only (lost when started by double-click); only panics reach `<user folder>/crash.log`, which the GUI never mentions. Write a (size-limited) log file to the user folder and add "Help > Open log folder" so users can attach both to bug reports. (Default log level is `info` now, was `debug`.)
- [ ] **Security: bump `quick-xml` to >= 0.41 in bioformats-rs** (RUSTSEC-2026-0194/0195: quadratic time / unbounded allocation on crafted XML - every opened image's metadata goes through it). Same fork: `lru` via `zarrs` is flagged unsound (RUSTSEC-2026-0253).
- [x] **Image decoder can panic on real files, not just corrupt ones** — fixed: `nr_bits == 0` is rejected with a clean error before the decode path (`image_reader.rs`) — [`crates/core/src/image/image_reader.rs:970-984,1034-1036`](crates/core/src/image/image_reader.rs#L970-L1036)
  If per-resolution metadata omits `BitsPerPixel` (plausible for pyramid sub-levels in real-world files), `nr_bits` defaults to 0 (`image_meta.rs:13` / `image_ome_parser.rs:251-254`). The decode path then calls `buffer.par_chunks_exact(0)` (panics unconditionally) or divides by `bytes_per_sample * source_planes` (divide-by-zero) — hard panic on open.
  *Fix:* validate `nr_bits != 0` after metadata parsing, fall back or return a clean `InternalErrors` instead of reaching the decode path.

- [x] **StarDist panics on NaN model output** — fixed: both sorts use `partial_cmp(..).unwrap_or(Ordering::Equal)` — [`crates/core/src/algos/ai_segmentation/stardist.rs:341,395`](crates/core/src/algos/ai_segmentation/stardist.rs#L341)
  `partial_cmp().unwrap()` in both the polygon scanline x-crossing sort and NMS score sort will panic if the model emits NaN (degenerate/blank tile, incompatible model export) — kills the whole segmentation job. Zero test coverage on this file.
  *Fix:* use `partial_cmp(...).unwrap_or(Ordering::Equal)` or filter NaN candidates before sorting; add tests.

- [x] **No crash visibility for beta users** — `crates/bin/src/main.rs:4-24`
  Logging is `env_logger`→stderr only; nothing when double-clicked as a packaged app. No `std::panic::set_hook` anywhere in `gui`/`app`/`bin`. Combined with the next item, a crash is completely silent and unreportable — beta feedback will be "it just closed" with no way to diagnose it.
  *Fix:* add a file log sink and a panic hook that writes the panic message/backtrace to a log file the user can attach to a bug report.

  - [x] (fixed: sizes are checked and a mismatch is an error) `ImageMath` silently truncates output on mismatched cached-image sizes (`.zip()`s two differently-sized slices with no check) instead of erroring — silent wrong output, not a crash — [`crates/core/src/algos/math/image_math.rs`](crates/core/src/algos/math/image_math.rs).

  ## Performance

- [ ] **Canny is unparallelized and over-allocates** — [`edge_detection_canny.rs:106-156`](crates/core/src/algos/filters/edge_detection_canny.rs#L106)
  4 fresh full-frame buffers allocated per call; every stage (except hysteresis DFS) is per-pixel parallel but runs single-threaded.
  *Fix:* reusable scratch buffers, `par_iter_mut` for magnitude/direction/threshold, row-wise parallel NMS.

- [ ] **Hessian wastes half its compute and is unparallelized** — [`hessian.rs:113-164`](crates/core/src/algos/filters/hessian.rs#L113)
  Calls the gradient function 4 times; 3 outputs are discarded immediately (~half the convolution work wasted). Final eigenvalue loop is sequential, unlike the near-identical code in `structure_tensor.rs` which already uses `par_iter_mut`.

- [ ] RGBA→RGB strip allocates a second full buffer via `flat_map`/`collect` instead of in-place compaction — [`image_reader.rs:823-828`](crates/core/src/image/image_reader.rs#L823).
- [ ] `sync_channel::<PipelineCache>(4)` in job executor is a flat buffer count, not scaled to tile size — could hold several hundred MB extra in flight for large tiles. [`job_executor.rs:502,757`](crates/core/src/job/job_executor.rs#L502)
- [ ] `pipeline_cache.rs` `Scratchpad` allocates a fresh zero-filled buffer on every request instead of reusing one — [`pipeline_cache.rs:76-119`](crates/core/src/pipeline/pipeline_cache.rs#L76)
- [ ] Sequential per-pixel passes not using rayon, unlike sibling filters that already do:
  - [`color_filter.rs:126-157`](crates/core/src/algos/filters/color_filter.rs#L126) (RGB→HSV via bounds-checked `get_pixel`/`set_pixel`)
  - [`enhance_contrast.rs:206-286`](crates/core/src/algos/filters/enhance_contrast.rs#L206) and [`intensity_transform.rs:130-152`](crates/core/src/algos/filters/intensity_transform.rs#L130) (histogram + LUT passes)
  - [`rolling_ball.rs:99-190`](crates/core/src/algos/filters/rolling_ball.rs#L99) (erosion/dilation passes, row-independent)
- [ ] `cellpose.rs` `follow_flows` per-pixel Euler integration (up to 1000 iterations) is unparallelized despite independent pixel trajectories — [`cellpose.rs:252-269`](crates/core/src/algos/ai_segmentation/cellpose.rs#L252)
- [ ] `stardist.rs` non-max-suppression is O(n²) candidate comparisons — could reuse the spatial-grid approach already implemented in `voronoi.rs` — [`stardist.rs:368-411`](crates/core/src/algos/ai_segmentation/stardist.rs#L368)
- [x] GUI: `visible_channels.contains()` checked inside the innermost per-pixel loop, turning O(pixels×channels) into O(pixels×channels²) — [`viewport_worker.rs:557-577`](crates/gui/src/editor/viewport_worker.rs#L557)
- [x] GUI: screen-space compositing loop is a serial ~8M-iteration loop per 4K frame while neighboring code uses rayon — [`viewport_worker.rs:387-406`](crates/gui/src/editor/viewport_worker.rs#L387)
- [ ] GUI: `composite_object_instances` allocates a full viewport-sized buffer and always runs a full border scan even when no ROIs are visible — [`viewport_controller.rs:435-507,824-844`](crates/gui/src/editor/viewport_controller.rs#L435)

- [ ] **GUI: in-memory pyramid for large images without pyramid levels** — postponed (2026-10-12): large images are assumed to always come with a pyramid (SVS, NDPI, MRXS, CZI, OME-TIFF do); plain big TIFFs without one aren't a common use case.
  *Today:* such images are safe for RAM - when no stored level is near the zoom, the viewport reads the region in 2048×512 blocks and keeps every n-th pixel (`read_subsampled` in [`viewport_cache.rs`](crates/gui/src/editor/viewport_cache.rs)), so peak RAM stays ~450 MB instead of 8 GB. But every zoom level decodes the whole image again: on a 20,000×15,000 RGB slide (2 cores) opening takes ~26 s, zooming to 5 % ~12 s, to 25 % ~2.3 s.
  *To do if needed:* while the first full read runs, also build reduced copies (¼, ⅛, 1/16 … down to ~1000 px) and keep them in the overview cache; serve every zoom ≤ 25 % from them, read only the visible region at finer zooms. Has to be cancellable (image switched mid-build), kept per plane (z, t, z-projection each), and within the cache budget (drop the ¼ level for very large images, e.g. cap ~64 MB on a 4 GB machine).
  *Cost / gain (estimated):* ~1/12 of the image size in RAM (~75 MB for the slide above), no extra decode time, ~250–400 lines + tests. Open ~26 → ~13 s, zoom to 5 %/25 % → ~0.1 s.
  *Cheaper first step:* derive a coarser subsampled tile from a finer one already cached (e.g. step 20 from step 10) - ~50 lines, makes zooming out from what was loaded instant.
  *Measure with:* `examples/make_test_slide.rs --flat` + the `bench_viewport_whole_slide` test (see its doc comment).
- [ ] `rank_filter.rs` median/outlier filter uses naive per-pixel sorting instead of a sliding histogram; has an unimplemented `// For parallel loop` hook — [`rank_filter.rs:149-218`](crates/core/src/algos/filters/rank_filter.rs#L149)
- [ ] `Object::measure_intensities`/`overlaps` are scalar nested loops, not parallelized (low severity — object bboxes are usually small) — [`object.rs:634-755`](crates/core/src/object.rs#L634)
- [ ] GUI: command-picker filter rebuilds 5 `Vec`s on every keystroke with no debounce — low impact while the command list stays small — [`pipelines_controller.rs:1465-1502`](crates/gui/src/editor/pipelines_controller.rs#L1465)
- [ ] `save_image.rs` full-buffer `map().collect()` per save call is sequential — low severity, debug/checkpoint path only — [`save_image.rs:78-158`](crates/core/src/algos/math/save_image.rs#L78)



# Components

- [x] If group or command name is too long the question mark to the right is not shown any more
- [x] To organize group and command paramters allow to define a "parameter category/group" all parameters in same categeory get an own section in the parameter list in the ui. For algos this should be deifned using #cmdsmeta for group parameters an optional category field should be added which is editable by the user
- [x] Expand featute for commands in the list. If a command or group gets many parameters the list may get a little bit overloaded. An exapnd feature would be noce which when clicked opens the command/group settings in a bigger dialog which shows the paremeter better organized simialr to the project settings.

## Components – not started

- [ ] **Project templates with a setup wizard** ("ready-to-use application"; the main selling point - rare on the market: map channels and classes, check a few values, run, yet still reproducible and open to experts).
  `ProjectTemplate` gets its own parameters with bindings, the same mechanism as component parameters (`ComponentParameter` / bindings, applied before the run). Bindings point to steps in the pipelines (including component parameters) **and** to project settings. Two special kinds:
  - **Channel roles:** "Nuclear channel", "Spot channel" - the user picks which image channel that is.
  - **Class roles:** "Nuclei", "Spots" - keep, rename, or map to existing project classes.

  Loading the template opens a **setup wizard**: name, images, channel roles, class roles, two or three values - done. Afterwards the same parameters stay available as a **"Template settings" panel**, so changing something later doesn't mean opening the pipelines.
- [ ] **CLI `--set`** (after the template parameters): `evanalyzer analyze --template spots.evapt --images ./plate1 --set "Spot channel=2" --set "Spot size=3"` - sets template parameters by label, for servers and batch runs.
- [ ] **Locking by the template author** - postponed on purpose: editing inside a component is always allowed today (an edited library component is detected by `origin.content_hash`).

## Components – smaller gaps

- [ ] **Breakpoints inside components.** Disabled inside an opened component; a breakpoint on the component card stops after the whole component. Needs a step path ("step 3, inside it step 2") instead of one `StepId` (`PipelineTask::breakpoint`, `expand_step`).
- [x] **Nested components (components inside components).** Done: open/edit/create/expand nested components in the editor, hand classes and memory slots inward, library fingerprint and insert/update with inner components.
  - [x] 1. Open a nested component from an opened component: `open_component` is a stack of `OpenComponent` levels, the bar shows "Pipeline › ◆ Outer › ◆ Inner", "‹ Back" goes one level up ([`pipelines_controller.rs`](crates/gui/src/editor/pipelines_controller.rs)). Inside a viewed component nested ones are only viewed ("View" stays on their cards); "Edit" in the bar opens the first viewed level to edit. "Only this step" works inside a component (`clone_component_for_step` takes a `StepList`). "Expand" is hidden inside a component until 5. is done.
  - [x] 2. Indirect cycles: `would_contain_itself` checks at any depth; inside an opened component the picker leaves out the components containing it, and inserting one anyway (e.g. through a library component's inner components) is refused with a message.
  - [x] 3. "Create component" inside an opened component: `plan_component`/`create_component` take a `StepList`. The outer component's local classes / component memory only the moved steps use go into the new component (same numbers); used by its other steps too → refused (needs 4.). Outer parameters setting moved settings set them through new parameters of the inner component (same label, default, section). Only applied if the outer component still expands.
  - [x] 4. Pass the outer component's local classes / memory slots into an inner one. Done differently than first proposed (no alias table): a nested use runs in the scope of the pipeline-level use around it, its own classes and component slots renumbered apart at expansion (`renumber` in [`components.rs`](crates/core/src/job/components.rs)); a handed-in "L1" / "component:1" is then just that number. Its end (`UseEnd::Nested`) removes objects with only its own classes, strips them from the outer one's objects, frees its own slots. Inside an opened component the class dropdowns offer its classes. "Create component" inside a component makes a class other steps use too a parameter (unset in the new component, the outer one's step hands it in). Behavior change: a nested component's "all objects" now also sees the outer one's objects.
  - [x] 4b. "Create component" inside a component: a component memory slot other steps of the outer one use too becomes a parameter of the new one (bound to the image-address settings naming it, "none" on its own, the outer one's step hands "component:N" in). Refused only where a moved step uses the slot outside an address setting (a threshold's memory value source) - it can't be bound.
  - [x] 5. "Expand" inside an opened component: the inner component's classes/slots become the outer one's (numbered after its own, via core `renumbered`), outer parameters setting its parameters set the settings behind them. In a pipeline, what an expanded component hands a component inside it becomes project classes / pipeline slots too (was left as "L1").
  - [x] 6. `content_hash` covers the components used inside (unchanged for one using none). Insert/update from the library: an inner component the project has with the same key but other content (changed here, or unrelated) stays - the library's is added under its own key ("INNER_2").
  - [x] 7. ◇/◆ on a component card inside an opened component: exposes its parameter on the outer component (`step_setting`), the card marks it, editing it sets the outer parameter's default.
- [ ] **Shift-click step selection** for "Create component" - today a from/to range dialog (the step list has no multi-selection).
- [ ] **"New empty component"** - not built; rarely needed since "Create component" exists.
- [ ] **Run the component checks on save** - `check_components` runs on load ([`project_ext.rs`](crates/app/src/workspace/extensions/project_ext.rs)) and in the editor, not on save. Runs still refuse a broken component at expansion, so mostly cosmetic.
- [ ] **LLM prompt doesn't know components** (`crates/llm`, unfinished anyway).
- [ ] **Check the picker's component look on screen** (◆ Components chip, component colour, "used N×", Library section) - never looked at, no headless screenshot in the tests.
- [ ] Optional, only if someone needs it: "don't merge this local class" at the tile merge - `classes_to_not_merge` only knows project classes, so local objects are always merged; would need a setting in the component definition.
## AI plugin

Load libtorch only on first AI use, from a plugin library (`cdylib`) next to the executable. Measured 2026-10-15 (Linux, release GUI): ~377 MB RSS right after start, of which libtorch is ~117 MB (CPU) and ~165 MB more with the CUDA libs (`libcublasLt` alone has an 81 MB `.bss` that is filled at load). Loading it takes ~0.2 s (CPU) / ~0.3 s (CUDA) with warm disk cache - likely seconds on a cold start. Today it is linked into the binary, so every user pays this at startup even without using AI. Same pattern as ONNX Runtime execution providers, ggml backends, Blender's GPU kernels.

*Boundary:* all libtorch work already sits inside the four `run_with_model` calls ([`unet.rs:120`](crates/core/src/algos/ai_segmentation/unet.rs#L120), [`cellpose.rs:129`](crates/core/src/algos/ai_segmentation/cellpose.rs#L129), [`stardist.rs:92`](crates/core/src/algos/ai_segmentation/stardist.rs#L92), [`yolov5.rs:122`](crates/core/src/algos/ai_segmentation/yolov5.rs#L122)), which return only plain data. Their closure bodies + `TorchScheduler` move into the plugin; pre/post-processing stays in core.

*Crates:*
- `crates/ai_api` (new, no libtorch): `API_VERSION`, serde types `InferRequest`/`InferResponse` (one variant per model), `InitConfig` (devices, gpu_slots, cores), `DeviceInfo`, `#[repr(C)] Buf { ptr, len }`, the C function signatures.
- `crates/ai_plugin` (new, `cdylib`, the only crate depending on `tch`): [`model_cache.rs`](crates/core/src/algos/ai_segmentation/model_cache.rs) scheduler (device slots, per-device model cache, OOM retry) + the four model inference functions + the torch tests (`test_support`, scheduler tests, `cellpose_sam_real_model.rs`). Built twice from the same source: `LIBTORCH=<cpu>` and `LIBTORCH=<cuda>`.
- `crates/core`: no `tch` dependency. New `ai_runtime` module (`OnceLock<Result<Plugin, String>>`, `libloading`); each `run_with_model(...)` becomes `ai_runtime::infer(InferRequest::.., input)`. smartcore/burn classifiers stay in core.

*C interface:*
```c
u32  eva_ai_api_version(void);
i32  eva_ai_init(Buf init_config, LogFn log, Buf* out_err);       // once
i32  eva_ai_devices(Buf* out_device_info, Buf* out_err);
i32  eva_ai_infer(Buf request, const f32* input, usize input_len,
                  Buf* out_response, Buf* out_err);               // thread-safe
void eva_ai_free(Buf buf);                                        // plugin frees its own memory
```
Input images by pointer (no copy), params/results as postcard bytes, `catch_unwind` around every export, log callback forwarded into the app's `log`. `eva_ai_infer` is called from many rayon workers at once; the plugin's scheduler blocks as today.

*Loading:* only from next to the executable (no system path - DLL hijacking). `--ai-devices cpu` → `ai/cpu` only (CUDA libs never loaded); `auto`/GPU list → `ai/cuda` if installed and a GPU is found, else `ai/cpu`. Separate folders are required (both ship a different `libtorch_cpu` under the same name). Linux: rpath `$ORIGIN`. Windows: `LoadLibraryExW` with `LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_DLL_LOAD_DIR | LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_DEFAULT_DIRS`. Check `api_version` before `init`. Missing/failed plugin → clean "AI support not installed / failed to load: …" error on the AI step, not a crash.

*Watch out:*
- The GUI's startup CUDA probe (`cuda_is_available` → [`gui/src/lib.rs:638`](crates/gui/src/lib.rs#L638), [`resources.rs:252`](crates/core/src/utils/resources.rs#L252)) must not go through the plugin, or libtorch is loaded at startup again. Replace with a driver probe (`libcuda.so.1`/`nvcuda.dll`: `cuInit` + `cuDeviceGetCount`) + "is `ai/cuda` installed".
- The `torch_cuda` relink hack in [`bin/build.rs`](crates/bin/build.rs) should become unnecessary: the CUDA plugin loads `libtorch_cuda`/`torch_cuda.dll` by path in `eva_ai_init` before the first torch call. Unverified - check on Linux and Windows.
- Once loaded, libtorch can't be unloaded - memory is deferred, not saved, for AI users.
- No crash isolation: a libtorch segfault still takes down the app.
- macOS: the plugin dylibs must be signed/notarized too.
- Maybe later: the CUDA libtorch contains a full `libtorch_cpu`, so one plugin build might serve both variants (only the shipped libtorch differs). Untested - start with two builds.

*Steps (each one testable):*
- [ ] 1. Pure refactor in core: turn each `run_with_model` closure into a named function with plain input/output types; no behavior change.
- [ ] 2. Create `ai_api` + `ai_plugin`, move those functions and `TorchScheduler` there; core links it as a normal Rust dependency first (tests the boundary types without dynamic loading).
- [ ] 3. Switch to `cdylib` + `ai_runtime` loader; core tests find the plugin via `EVANALYZER_AI_PLUGIN`.
- [ ] 4. Replace the CUDA probe with the driver probe; remove the `bin/build.rs` relink.
- [ ] 5. Installers: base app + `ai/cpu` or `ai/cuda`; smoke test "no plugin", "CPU plugin", "CUDA plugin without GPU". Measure startup RSS/time before and after.

## UI component library

Done with the Ink redesign (2026-10-17): the old `components/` folder is gone - every dialog uses `InkDialog` (About is custom on purpose), buttons, fields, chips, menus, rows, settings rows, tabs and dividers come from `crates/gui/ui/ink/`. This round: `InkFact` (object card + objects panel), plain dividers → `InkDivider`, the AI dialog's private header/divider → `InkCaption`/`InkDivider`, dead `CategoryChip` removed (generated code −12 KB).

Kept private on purpose (one screen's own, not a copy of an Ink piece): the step picker's category header and filter chips (category colours), pipeline step / parameter rows, the plate's wells, About's cards. `FormLabel` (AI training, component dialogs) stays a plain `Text` in Ink's style: `InkLabel`'s elide costs ~2 KB per use, 35 uses.

- [ ] Lower priority: `std-widgets` `ScrollView` (14×) / `ListView` (6×) / `TextEdit` (2×) are still Slint's own - an Ink scroll bar would make scrolling look the same everywhere.
